<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Palatinate (region)</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Rhenish Palatinate, Lower Palatinate, Bavarian Palatinate</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Palatinate (Pfalz) is a historical region of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> on the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. It took its name from the counts palatine of the Rhine, Wittelsbach princes from 1214, whose lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mannheim">Mannheim</a> and the left bank of the Rhine were called the Rhenish or Lower Palatinate to distinguish them from the Upper Palatinate in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. Its western part lies today in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/palatinate-region.jpg" alt="Palatinate (region)" width="320" height="399" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maximilian Dörrbecker (Chumwa) · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Teilbereiche_der_Pfalz.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
